nodecode-phone-call is a rewrite of winchxyz's winchxyz/hermes-phone:

  https://github.com/winchxyz/hermes-phone
  commit 0ef88344b16a19db160df84e6857eb329c2dd7f4
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 winchxyz (LICENSE)

The plugin was four Hermes tools (phone_prepare, phone_call, phone_status,
phone_transcript), a /phone slash command with seven verbs, a skill, three
hooks, and a FastAPI bridge that carried a live call: Twilio Media Streams
piped mu-law audio to and from an OpenAI Realtime session, the bridge
transcribed both legs, gated what the voice model could see behind a per-call
consent model, and posted a question card to the operator's chat when the
callee asked something the brief did not cover. This rewrite keeps, in Common
Lisp, the part a person relies on: the brief and its approval card, the AI
disclosure as the first sentence of every call, the one-use fifteen-minute
approval checked before every dial, the hard blocklist of emergency and
premium numbers, the fail-closed allowlist the operator sets in the section,
the opt-in quiet hours, one live call and the daily and per-number limits, the
sensitive-field consent model with its never-shared cvv, password and
one-time code, the drafting of the opening line, the cost estimate, and the
four tool names and jobs. The bridge became TwiML: the call speaks the
disclosure and the approved message, records the answer when the operator
asked for it, and the transcript is what the cell said plus Twilio's recording
link. What was left out, and why: the realtime voice session and its media
bridge, the live transcription of both legs, the mid-call question card and
the polite hold (a cell has no public URL to hand Twilio and no audio device),
the web-phone mode that let a browser play the callee, the plain-reply hooks
that answered "call" in chat, the SQLite store shared by two processes (one
process serves this rewrite, so a call is a plist in memory), and the
post-call result message the bridge posted by itself. Nothing of the original
is vendored; the Twilio REST calls, the TwiML document, the base64 and
form encoding and the state machine are written here.
